1
00:00:04,004 --> 00:00:06,272
<i>An express bus fell off a bridge.</i>

2
00:00:06,339 --> 00:00:10,510
<i>Twelve have lost their lives.</i>
<i>One male passenger is still missing.</i>

3
00:00:10,577 --> 00:00:13,413
<i>Your husband was having an affair</i>
<i>with my wife.</i>

4
00:00:14,614 --> 00:00:16,383
<i>They were sitting next to each other.</i>

5
00:00:17,283 --> 00:00:18,718
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

6
00:00:18,785 --> 00:00:21,588
You're just the same height as him.

7
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:32,265
NAGANO PREFECTURAL POLICE

8
00:00:48,515 --> 00:00:49,349
Hello.

9
00:00:52,085 --> 00:00:53,286
That's right.

10
00:00:57,257 --> 00:00:58,091
Yes.

11
00:01:02,228 --> 00:01:03,063
What?

12
00:01:05,965 --> 00:01:07,200
Yes, of course.

13
00:01:08,234 --> 00:01:09,436
Thank you, bye.

14
00:01:16,142 --> 00:01:18,344
They say the search has been called off.

15
00:01:19,512 --> 00:01:20,914
That means…

16
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:22,248
Mitsuru

17
00:01:23,116 --> 00:01:25,285
was apparently not on the bus.

18
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:28,221
What?

19
00:01:32,258 --> 00:01:33,993
Please take a look at this.

20
00:01:39,132 --> 00:01:40,700
The man who just got on.

21
00:01:41,501 --> 00:01:43,002
Yes, that's my husband.

22
00:01:45,872 --> 00:01:46,973
And here.

23
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,242
The same man is getting off.

24
00:01:50,076 --> 00:01:50,910
What?

25
00:01:51,744 --> 00:01:55,782
Right after this,
the bus departs without him returning.

26
00:01:57,417 --> 00:01:58,818
That means,

27
00:01:58,885 --> 00:02:01,888
my husband wasn't on the bus
at the time of the accident.

28
00:02:02,655 --> 00:02:06,693
His luggage was still there,
and he seemed flustered,

29
00:02:06,759 --> 00:02:09,996
so I think there must have been
some reason for getting off.

30
00:02:10,063 --> 00:02:11,698
Before he got back on,

31
00:02:11,764 --> 00:02:16,503
security cameras captured him
buying something at the rest area,

32
00:02:16,569 --> 00:02:18,905
but he didn't seem to behave
out of the ordinary.

33
00:02:19,772 --> 00:02:21,608
He got off on his own.

34
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:24,344
Why?

35
00:02:26,679 --> 00:02:28,715
Maybe he was trying to save himself.

36
00:02:31,084 --> 00:02:33,987
I mean, right after that,
the crash happened.

37
00:02:34,888 --> 00:02:36,990
Are you saying Mitsuru killed them?

38
00:02:37,056 --> 00:02:39,025
-How?
-No, that's not what I'm saying.

39
00:02:39,092 --> 00:02:41,427
I just thought it looked that way.

40
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:44,130
Anyway…

41
00:02:44,797 --> 00:02:47,700
now that we know
he got off the bus on his own,

42
00:02:47,767 --> 00:02:50,336
we're calling off the search
at the accident site.

43
00:02:51,171 --> 00:02:52,005
Right.

44
00:02:52,572 --> 00:02:55,341
But you'll still be
looking for him, right?

45
00:02:55,408 --> 00:02:57,210
Like asking around at the rest area?

46
00:02:57,277 --> 00:03:00,880
There are no signs of a crime,
so we can't conduct an active search.

47
00:03:00,947 --> 00:03:04,284
But he hasn't come home.

48
00:03:05,351 --> 00:03:07,253
He might have collapsed somewhere.

49
00:03:07,954 --> 00:03:09,989
Is it possible that he ran away?

50
00:03:11,824 --> 00:03:13,059
Ran away?

51
00:03:13,560 --> 00:03:15,461
It happens a lot. Those who elope…

52
00:03:16,496 --> 00:03:20,533
I mean, people who try to escape
their current lives and disappear.

53
00:03:23,670 --> 00:03:26,906
We'll be preparing a statement,
so please wait here.

54
00:03:27,607 --> 00:03:28,708
Excuse us.

55
00:03:35,048 --> 00:03:36,282
I'm sorry.

56
00:03:37,650 --> 00:03:38,618
It's okay.

57
00:03:38,685 --> 00:03:41,988
It's not like he was trying to save
himself or anything. I'm really sorry.

58
00:03:51,598 --> 00:03:53,333
Should we rent a car nearby?

59
00:03:55,735 --> 00:03:56,769
Rent a car?

60
00:03:56,836 --> 00:03:58,738
To get to the rest area.

61
00:03:58,805 --> 00:04:00,807
I'm not so keen on taking the bus.

62
00:04:04,244 --> 00:04:05,278
I mean,

63
00:04:06,045 --> 00:04:08,014
your husband is probably still alive.

64
00:04:09,182 --> 00:04:10,717
We have to look for him.

65
00:04:15,154 --> 00:04:19,058
Azusa's death was an accident
caused by the driver's sudden illness.

66
00:04:19,125 --> 00:04:21,894
It's frustrating, but I've accepted that.

67
00:04:23,596 --> 00:04:24,631
But still,

68
00:04:25,198 --> 00:04:26,833
why she got on that bus,

69
00:04:27,467 --> 00:04:29,769
and why her travel partner was gone,

70
00:04:30,370 --> 00:04:33,573
those are the things
I want to fully understand.

71
00:04:36,075 --> 00:04:37,110
Right now,

72
00:04:37,176 --> 00:04:39,579
I want to see your husband
more than you do.

73
00:04:42,081 --> 00:04:45,151
Even if we do see him,
please don't punch him right away.

74
00:04:45,985 --> 00:04:47,320
Maybe I will.

75
00:04:47,387 --> 00:04:48,354
No, you won't!

76
00:04:50,089 --> 00:04:51,457
I have to punch him first.

77
00:04:51,524 --> 00:04:53,493
Oh, is that how you want it?

78
00:04:55,495 --> 00:04:57,930
I most certainly want to see him
more than you do.

79
00:04:59,198 --> 00:05:01,234
Well, that's fine then.

80
00:05:12,245 --> 00:05:13,946
-Yes.
-Thank you, sir.

81
00:05:14,013 --> 00:05:15,815
-Please drive safely.
-Got it.

82
00:05:27,327 --> 00:05:28,328
What's wrong?

83
00:05:29,295 --> 00:05:30,296
Nothing.

84
00:05:31,564 --> 00:05:33,399
Can I make a quick phone call?

85
00:05:33,466 --> 00:05:34,367
Sure.

86
00:05:41,274 --> 00:05:42,909
Hello, Naoto.

87
00:05:44,177 --> 00:05:45,578
I see.

88
00:05:46,713 --> 00:05:47,647
That's great.

89
00:05:49,449 --> 00:05:50,783
Or maybe not.

90
00:05:52,218 --> 00:05:54,287
<i>It's hard to know how to react, right?</i>

91
00:05:54,354 --> 00:05:58,024
<i>But the chances that he's alive</i>
<i>have gone up by a lot.</i>

92
00:05:58,091 --> 00:06:00,426
<i>I just thought I should let you know.</i>

93
00:06:01,961 --> 00:06:02,995
Thank you.

94
00:06:03,496 --> 00:06:06,065
Let me know if you hear from Mitsuru.

95
00:06:06,132 --> 00:06:09,535
<i>If he's in a position to contact me,</i>
<i>he would call you first.</i>

96
00:06:10,570 --> 00:06:11,804
Not necessarily.

97
00:06:12,405 --> 00:06:13,506
<i>For Mitsuru</i>

98
00:06:13,573 --> 00:06:16,309
<i>to put me above all else in everything,</i>

99
00:06:17,310 --> 00:06:18,678
<i>that's so not like him.</i>

100
00:06:20,279 --> 00:06:22,548
If anything happens,
I'll contact you right away.

101
00:06:23,416 --> 00:06:24,317
Thank you.

102
00:06:25,051 --> 00:06:27,086
<i>Sakiko, are you okay?</i>

103
00:06:27,887 --> 00:06:29,288
<i>Eat well, will you?</i>

104
00:06:29,355 --> 00:06:30,189
Mrs. Seo!

105
00:06:31,624 --> 00:06:33,025
<i>I've set the GPS.</i>

106
00:06:33,092 --> 00:06:34,794
Should we head out now?

107
00:06:35,294 --> 00:06:36,529
<i>Yes, of course.</i>

108
00:06:37,663 --> 00:06:40,533
<i>Sorry, Naoto, what did you say?</i>

109
00:06:41,134 --> 00:06:42,435
Are you going for a drive?

110
00:06:42,502 --> 00:06:43,836
<i>Oh, no.</i>

111
00:06:43,903 --> 00:06:47,073
I thought of going to the service area
where Mitsuru got off.

112
00:06:49,275 --> 00:06:50,676
Take care of yourself.

113
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:53,613
Thank you. Bye.

114
00:06:54,180 --> 00:06:55,114
Bye.

115
00:07:00,553 --> 00:07:02,955
Take care of yourself, seriously…

116
00:07:19,205 --> 00:07:20,773
-Sorry about that.
-It's okay.

117
00:07:21,641 --> 00:07:22,508
Hey.

118
00:07:23,142 --> 00:07:25,011
What happened to your smartphone?

119
00:07:25,077 --> 00:07:26,312
You mean, my wife's?

120
00:07:26,379 --> 00:07:27,313
Yes.

121
00:07:27,380 --> 00:07:29,749
It's broken. It won't turn on.

122
00:07:30,750 --> 00:07:31,951
Not getting it fixed?

123
00:07:32,585 --> 00:07:35,788
Why, so we can pry into it for clues?

124
00:07:40,293 --> 00:07:42,829
Does your husband still have his phone?

125
00:07:43,930 --> 00:07:46,365
It seems he left it on the bus.

126
00:07:47,600 --> 00:07:50,803
It's completely wrecked,
so don't count on it.

127
00:07:51,370 --> 00:07:52,872
You won't have it fixed?

128
00:07:59,378 --> 00:08:00,913
If the phone worked,

129
00:08:01,481 --> 00:08:03,850
I would be scared to find something.

130
00:08:04,550 --> 00:08:06,686
And if I found nothing,
I would feel guilty.

131
00:08:06,752 --> 00:08:08,087
I get that.

132
00:08:08,855 --> 00:08:11,123
I was actually relieved
it wouldn't turn on.

133
00:08:11,190 --> 00:08:13,025
I know, right?

134
00:08:14,393 --> 00:08:17,663
But I guess the stuff they left behind

135
00:08:18,364 --> 00:08:20,266
feels more credible.

136
00:08:20,333 --> 00:08:24,070
What, you don't trust
other people's testimonies?

137
00:08:25,538 --> 00:08:30,109
I just can't quite wrap my head around
what Naoto said to me.

138
00:08:31,611 --> 00:08:34,447
I can't imagine Mitsuru
complaining to him about me.

139
00:08:34,514 --> 00:08:35,748
Wow.

140
00:08:35,815 --> 00:08:38,184
You're very sure about that.

141
00:08:38,251 --> 00:08:41,287
I'm sure he had some kind of grievance.

142
00:08:41,354 --> 00:08:45,157
It's just that I can't imagine him
venting to Naoto.

143
00:08:45,725 --> 00:08:46,659
Why?

144
00:08:47,159 --> 00:08:50,663
Mitsuru is very considerate
of other people's perspectives.

145
00:08:51,464 --> 00:08:53,165
As an employer,

146
00:08:53,232 --> 00:08:55,868
he considers inconveniencing people,
even over trivial stuff,

147
00:08:55,935 --> 00:08:57,570
as power harassment.

148
00:08:58,638 --> 00:08:59,672
That's why,

149
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:03,442
no matter how dissatisfied
he might be with me,

150
00:09:04,277 --> 00:09:06,846
I didn't think he would vent at Naoto.

151
00:09:09,148 --> 00:09:10,483
That's amazing.

152
00:09:12,151 --> 00:09:13,586
Indeed, he is.

153
00:09:14,687 --> 00:09:17,957
He's the kind of person
who always thinks about others.

154
00:09:18,858 --> 00:09:19,992
For that I respect him.

155
00:09:20,059 --> 00:09:21,494
That's not what I meant.

156
00:09:22,194 --> 00:09:24,764
As much as you praise your husband,

157
00:09:24,830 --> 00:09:27,166
can you really trust him that much?

158
00:09:28,034 --> 00:09:29,068
I see.

159
00:09:30,236 --> 00:09:33,739
You know the current state of affairs.
He missed the bus, right?

160
00:09:34,674 --> 00:09:37,843
Yet, he didn't come home,
so even before we can get into whether--

161
00:09:37,910 --> 00:09:40,079
He ran away from his wife?

162
00:09:46,519 --> 00:09:48,821
The reason I'm blabbering on like this

163
00:09:49,722 --> 00:09:52,224
is because if I stop thinking,
I might go crazy.

164
00:09:54,360 --> 00:09:57,096
Even so, I believe in Mitsuru.

165
00:09:58,030 --> 00:09:59,632
Believe in what, exactly?

166
00:09:59,699 --> 00:10:02,602
That he's not having an affair,
or that he didn't run away?

167
00:10:03,502 --> 00:10:05,104
Both.

168
00:10:06,706 --> 00:10:08,374
I mean, Mitsuru…

169
00:10:09,208 --> 00:10:11,978
He proposed to me, didn't he?

170
00:10:13,079 --> 00:10:15,014
He asked me to marry him.

171
00:10:16,616 --> 00:10:18,150
For crying out loud.

172
00:10:35,534 --> 00:10:37,870
I've been here before.

173
00:10:39,572 --> 00:10:41,674
With the man who proposed to you?

174
00:10:42,942 --> 00:10:44,176
How did you know?

175
00:10:44,910 --> 00:10:46,412
I could tell by your face.

176
00:10:48,681 --> 00:10:49,715
By my face?

177
00:10:50,383 --> 00:10:54,820
The security camera caught him
shopping somewhere, didn't it?

178
00:10:55,421 --> 00:10:56,422
Um…

179
00:10:56,489 --> 00:11:00,426
It was a curry bread specialty shop,
and he bought two, they said.

180
00:11:00,493 --> 00:11:01,727
Should we check it out?

181
00:11:02,461 --> 00:11:03,696
Do you have a photo?

182
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:05,698
Right.

183
00:11:06,632 --> 00:11:09,902
How about this one?
It's from about two months ago.

184
00:11:12,038 --> 00:11:13,339
How about this one?

185
00:11:14,073 --> 00:11:16,742
-Something like this?
-The first one is better.

186
00:11:18,044 --> 00:11:18,944
Okay.

187
00:11:22,581 --> 00:11:23,516
Oh, look.

188
00:11:24,350 --> 00:11:25,551
Is this the place?

189
00:11:26,352 --> 00:11:27,286
Yeah…

190
00:11:29,689 --> 00:11:31,757
They only have one item on the menu.

191
00:11:33,492 --> 00:11:35,728
Want to grab one? I sure do.

192
00:11:40,066 --> 00:11:41,067
Mrs. Seo.

193
00:11:45,171 --> 00:11:46,839
I'll have one.

194
00:11:47,840 --> 00:11:48,741
Well…

195
00:11:49,375 --> 00:11:50,509
it was to be expected.

196
00:11:51,277 --> 00:11:55,314
With hundreds of customers each day,
there's no way they'd remember every face.

197
00:11:56,082 --> 00:11:57,416
So, now what?

198
00:11:59,285 --> 00:12:01,454
Should we ask at the other shops?

199
00:12:05,558 --> 00:12:07,193
Are you even listening?

200
00:12:10,663 --> 00:12:11,630
This…

201
00:12:13,933 --> 00:12:15,434
I ate it with Mitsuru, too.

202
00:12:16,402 --> 00:12:17,336
Really?

203
00:12:18,037 --> 00:12:18,871
It's pumpkin.

204
00:12:18,938 --> 00:12:20,473
I can see that.

205
00:12:20,539 --> 00:12:22,775
Did Azusa like pumpkin?

206
00:12:23,342 --> 00:12:24,710
Yes, she did.

207
00:12:25,945 --> 00:12:26,946
I see.

208
00:12:28,047 --> 00:12:28,881
Yes.

209
00:12:30,349 --> 00:12:32,051
Once we were both having this,

210
00:12:32,118 --> 00:12:35,154
and we got all excited, saying,
"Wow, there's pumpkin!"

211
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:37,490
You don't like pumpkin?

212
00:12:39,859 --> 00:12:41,127
I like it, actually.

213
00:12:41,994 --> 00:12:43,496
It's Mitsuru who doesn't.

214
00:12:43,562 --> 00:12:44,663
I see.

215
00:12:45,431 --> 00:12:47,233
Then why did he buy it again?

216
00:12:48,567 --> 00:12:50,603
I assume he bought it for Azusa.

217
00:12:54,373 --> 00:12:58,377
I kept saying how delicious it was
until I gobbled up Mitsuru's portion.

218
00:12:59,311 --> 00:13:02,782
He probably knew that
pumpkin lovers will find it delicious.

219
00:13:04,750 --> 00:13:06,218
But he bought two, right?

220
00:13:08,087 --> 00:13:10,656
<i>Maybe he just forgot</i>
<i>that these have pumpkin in it?</i>

221
00:13:12,124 --> 00:13:13,092
Right.

222
00:13:16,996 --> 00:13:19,498
He probably got one for himself
and one for Azusa.

223
00:13:22,401 --> 00:13:25,037
And he had forgotten
about eating these with me.

224
00:13:30,976 --> 00:13:32,044
That was delicious.

225
00:13:32,111 --> 00:13:33,546
I'll buy some to take home.

226
00:13:42,822 --> 00:13:46,592
Sorry, now you even have to go through
the trouble of returning the car.

227
00:13:46,659 --> 00:13:49,728
No, I was planning to drive this
straight to my parents'.

228
00:13:49,795 --> 00:13:50,796
I see.

229
00:13:50,863 --> 00:13:52,531
I'm glad to hear that.

230
00:13:53,833 --> 00:13:55,201
Does Sho like pumpkin?

231
00:13:55,835 --> 00:13:57,403
Yes, he likes it.

232
00:13:57,469 --> 00:13:59,839
Then, give him this as a souvenir.

233
00:13:59,905 --> 00:14:01,307
No, that's yours.

234
00:14:01,373 --> 00:14:04,777
I would be happy
if someone who likes pumpkin could eat it.

235
00:14:04,844 --> 00:14:06,912
-Well then, thank you.
-Yes.

236
00:14:07,746 --> 00:14:08,747
Well…

237
00:14:09,715 --> 00:14:12,785
If I find something on the phone,
I'll let you know.

238
00:14:13,619 --> 00:14:14,720
What?

239
00:14:14,787 --> 00:14:15,955
I'm going to get it fixed.

240
00:14:16,021 --> 00:14:17,323
Wow.

241
00:14:18,224 --> 00:14:19,658
I won't make you have hers fixed.

242
00:14:19,725 --> 00:14:20,759
Okay.

243
00:14:22,728 --> 00:14:23,696
All right.

244
00:14:24,263 --> 00:14:25,464
Thank you so much.

245
00:14:25,531 --> 00:14:26,966
No problem.

246
00:14:27,032 --> 00:14:28,067
Take care.

247
00:14:31,971 --> 00:14:33,072
"Take care"…

248
00:14:36,141 --> 00:14:41,780
YAMURAMACHI STATION

249
00:15:09,108 --> 00:15:10,075
Sorry.

250
00:15:11,911 --> 00:15:14,446
I got in a car, sitting next to the driver

251
00:15:16,015 --> 00:15:17,483
who's a man.

252
00:15:18,751 --> 00:15:20,486
You thought if you sat in the back,

253
00:15:22,621 --> 00:15:24,657
it would seem like
you were being too polite

254
00:15:25,257 --> 00:15:27,593
and make him feel like
he had to be considerate.

255
00:15:28,794 --> 00:15:29,662
Yes.

256
00:15:31,130 --> 00:15:33,565
I was torn about it when I got in.

257
00:15:33,632 --> 00:15:36,568
You're so kind,
always thinking about others.

258
00:15:39,238 --> 00:15:40,940
Do you actually like pumpkin?

259
00:15:41,740 --> 00:15:43,642
No. I'm not a fan.

260
00:15:44,910 --> 00:15:45,945
I thought so.

261
00:15:46,578 --> 00:15:47,613
You know,

262
00:15:50,249 --> 00:15:53,686
that time when there was about
one serving of curry left in the pot,

263
00:15:54,653 --> 00:15:55,721
so I…

264
00:15:56,555 --> 00:15:58,891
microwaved some bite-sized
frozen pumpkin cubes,

265
00:15:58,958 --> 00:16:00,793
added them to the curry, and ate it.

266
00:16:01,860 --> 00:16:04,530
I didn't know that. Was it good?

267
00:16:05,631 --> 00:16:06,632
Yes.

268
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:09,768
It tasted good, but…

269
00:16:10,936 --> 00:16:13,872
I felt I had to finish it
before you got home,

270
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:16,008
so I ate it in a hurry.

271
00:16:16,075 --> 00:16:19,345
Why? You didn't do anything wrong.

272
00:16:19,411 --> 00:16:21,613
Yes, but…

273
00:16:22,614 --> 00:16:25,084
I made it on the sly
and ate it on the sly.

274
00:16:26,819 --> 00:16:28,320
It's something I like,

275
00:16:29,388 --> 00:16:31,156
but you don't.

276
00:16:34,727 --> 00:16:37,129
It was a curry
tailored to both of our tastes,

277
00:16:38,430 --> 00:16:39,498
that we made together

278
00:16:40,399 --> 00:16:42,368
and ate together.

279
00:16:45,204 --> 00:16:47,239
So when I did that with the leftovers…

280
00:16:50,342 --> 00:16:52,011
I knew it wasn't wrong,

281
00:16:54,446 --> 00:16:56,148
but I felt like I had to hide it.

282
00:16:58,550 --> 00:16:59,752
I see.

283
00:17:03,522 --> 00:17:04,890
When you were

284
00:17:05,958 --> 00:17:07,726
meeting Azusa every month,

285
00:17:09,962 --> 00:17:11,563
did you feel the same?

286
00:17:39,725 --> 00:17:41,460
-Sho.
-Daddy!

287
00:17:41,527 --> 00:17:47,433
Fist bump, fist bump, high-five, hug!

288
00:17:47,499 --> 00:17:48,734
Have you been a good boy?

289
00:17:48,801 --> 00:17:50,335
-Yes.
-Good.

290
00:17:50,402 --> 00:17:51,437
Look.

291
00:17:51,503 --> 00:17:52,771
What is it?

292
00:17:53,338 --> 00:17:54,173
What?

293
00:17:54,239 --> 00:17:56,575
This isn't the one I gave you
for your birthday.

294
00:17:57,209 --> 00:17:59,378
She bought this for me
because I'm a good boy.

295
00:18:02,614 --> 00:18:03,782
I'm sorry.

296
00:18:04,716 --> 00:18:06,552
You're already busy taking care of Dad.

297
00:18:06,618 --> 00:18:07,619
It's fine.

298
00:18:08,587 --> 00:18:10,522
Daddy, when are you coming back again?

299
00:18:10,589 --> 00:18:13,292
We'll be going back to our home in Tokyo.

300
00:18:13,358 --> 00:18:14,426
Hey, Sho.

301
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:16,628
Daddy is driving today.

302
00:18:17,162 --> 00:18:18,730
Wanna ride home with me?

303
00:18:18,797 --> 00:18:20,999
Not in a car
you don't know who's been in it.

304
00:18:21,066 --> 00:18:23,502
Sho, let's go take a bath.

305
00:18:23,569 --> 00:18:25,971
-Get your pajamas ready.
-Okay.

306
00:18:30,843 --> 00:18:34,079
Don't try to win his affection
by buying him things.

307
00:18:34,146 --> 00:18:35,881
Azusa didn't like that, either.

308
00:18:36,548 --> 00:18:40,252
I just felt bad for Sho
because Azusa had been unnaturally strict.

309
00:18:40,319 --> 00:18:41,753
It's just normal discipline.

310
00:18:42,454 --> 00:18:44,256
Every family is like that.

311
00:18:44,323 --> 00:18:46,058
She wouldn't know what normal is.

312
00:18:46,125 --> 00:18:48,360
She wasn't raised in a normal household.

313
00:18:49,461 --> 00:18:51,497
-That's exactly my point.
-What?

314
00:18:51,563 --> 00:18:54,833
Have you ever talked to Azusa herself
about the environment she grew up in?

315
00:18:56,768 --> 00:18:57,669
I don't remember.

316
00:18:57,736 --> 00:18:59,204
Are you just being insensitive--

317
00:18:59,271 --> 00:19:01,206
What's that? A souvenir?

318
00:19:01,273 --> 00:19:02,207
What?

319
00:19:02,274 --> 00:19:05,277
I got curry bread to give to Sho.

320
00:19:05,344 --> 00:19:07,479
From the woman you went out with?

321
00:19:08,280 --> 00:19:09,114
What?

322
00:19:09,815 --> 00:19:13,685
You've got to be careful with that car.
A woman's scent may linger.

323
00:19:13,752 --> 00:19:15,521
That's not what's going on.

324
00:19:16,288 --> 00:19:20,826
For a five-year-old
to receive such greasy food…

325
00:19:20,893 --> 00:19:22,628
She doesn't have kids, does she?

326
00:19:23,529 --> 00:19:26,064
Isn't that great?
You could think about remarrying.

327
00:19:26,131 --> 00:19:27,299
Never mind.

328
00:19:27,866 --> 00:19:28,934
Ta-da!

329
00:19:29,001 --> 00:19:31,136
Hey, Sho.

330
00:19:32,137 --> 00:19:34,740
When shall we go back to Tokyo?
Maybe tomorrow?

331
00:19:35,507 --> 00:19:37,376
Is Mommy coming back tomorrow?

332
00:19:38,243 --> 00:19:40,279
I don't think she'll be back tomorrow.

333
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:41,847
Oh…

334
00:19:41,914 --> 00:19:43,415
Then, I want to stay here.

335
00:19:43,482 --> 00:19:44,550
What?

336
00:19:58,330 --> 00:20:00,799
SAKIKO SEO
THANK YOU FOR ACCOMPANYING ME TODAY.

337
00:20:00,866 --> 00:20:02,901
I REALLY APPRECIATE IT. NO NEED TO REPLY.

338
00:20:17,049 --> 00:20:18,283
-Hey.
-Hey.

339
00:20:18,784 --> 00:20:21,019
-Your kid still at your parents'?
-Yes.

340
00:20:22,054 --> 00:20:24,122
Instead of leaving him there
for the weekend,

341
00:20:24,189 --> 00:20:27,659
you should take him out somewhere,
before his grandma takes over.

342
00:20:27,726 --> 00:20:28,927
I guess you're right.

343
00:20:28,994 --> 00:20:30,429
He's five, right?

344
00:20:30,495 --> 00:20:33,131
It's too hot outside,
so I would recommend indoor places.

345
00:20:33,198 --> 00:20:35,467
What kind of indoor places can he play in?

346
00:20:35,534 --> 00:20:39,171
Like KidZania,
the Anpanman Children's Museum,

347
00:20:39,238 --> 00:20:40,839
or Sanrio Puroland.

348
00:20:40,906 --> 00:20:42,641
Hold on, let me jot those down.

349
00:20:42,708 --> 00:20:43,542
All right.

350
00:20:43,609 --> 00:20:46,845
Well, KidZania, Anpanman…

351
00:20:46,912 --> 00:20:48,947
Or an aquarium, to play it safe.

352
00:20:52,117 --> 00:20:53,151
Well…

353
00:20:53,218 --> 00:20:56,688
KidZania, Anpanman, what was it again?

354
00:20:56,755 --> 00:20:57,689
Puroland.

355
00:20:57,756 --> 00:21:00,192
The one with Hello Kitty.
So that's indoors?

356
00:21:00,259 --> 00:21:03,729
Yes, but I would still
recommend the aquarium.

357
00:21:03,795 --> 00:21:06,064
It's relaxing for adults,
and it's near the station.

358
00:21:06,131 --> 00:21:07,232
I see…

359
00:21:08,100 --> 00:21:10,569
I've never been to KidZania.
It sounds fun.

360
00:21:10,636 --> 00:21:13,805
Excuse me,
and thank you for your patience.

361
00:21:15,274 --> 00:21:16,341
Here you go.

362
00:21:18,577 --> 00:21:20,545
Thank you so much.

363
00:21:22,481 --> 00:21:24,349
What's the passcode for this?

364
00:21:24,416 --> 00:21:25,317
Well…

365
00:21:25,384 --> 00:21:27,886
I'm sorry,
but we don't unlock phones here.

366
00:21:27,953 --> 00:21:28,954
I see.

367
00:21:29,021 --> 00:21:30,522
I see. Thanks.

368
00:21:53,679 --> 00:21:55,647
First, his birthday…

369
00:21:55,714 --> 00:21:59,651
ENTER PASSCODE

370
00:22:04,690 --> 00:22:05,991
I'm scared.

371
00:22:10,696 --> 00:22:12,597
<i>So, you're stumped?</i>

372
00:22:13,165 --> 00:22:16,601
I tried his birthday and our wedding
anniversary, but they didn't work.

373
00:22:17,836 --> 00:22:19,171
What about you?

374
00:22:19,237 --> 00:22:20,138
What?

375
00:22:20,872 --> 00:22:22,474
What's your phone's passcode?

376
00:22:22,541 --> 00:22:23,575
Um…

377
00:22:24,476 --> 00:22:25,310
Wait…

378
00:22:26,945 --> 00:22:29,114
I won't try it, okay?

379
00:22:30,048 --> 00:22:34,753
I'm just gathering info on what
men base their four-digit passcodes on.

380
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:38,423
It's Sho's weight at birth: 3,075 grams.

381
00:22:38,490 --> 00:22:39,624
Oops, I said it.

382
00:22:39,691 --> 00:22:42,227
So, it's not your birthday?

383
00:22:42,294 --> 00:22:46,231
If I used the birth date,
others would likely find out easily.

384
00:22:46,298 --> 00:22:48,567
But birth weight? Not so much.

385
00:22:49,935 --> 00:22:53,138
I see, but your parents might know.

386
00:22:53,739 --> 00:22:56,241
I think Azusa
could have figured it out easily.

387
00:22:56,875 --> 00:22:58,977
So you're saying you have nothing to hide.

388
00:23:00,779 --> 00:23:03,949
It's also a way of showing that.

389
00:23:05,650 --> 00:23:06,518
Showing what?

390
00:23:06,585 --> 00:23:09,688
A password my wife
could figure out easily, you know.

391
00:23:10,355 --> 00:23:12,924
It doesn't seem like
I'm hiding anything, right?

392
00:23:13,625 --> 00:23:15,327
Besides, it's tied to our son.

393
00:23:16,695 --> 00:23:18,063
I see…

394
00:23:20,699 --> 00:23:22,200
So that means…

395
00:23:23,602 --> 00:23:26,071
Mitsuru is indeed…

396
00:23:31,410 --> 00:23:34,112
You've gotten pretty timid, haven't you?

397
00:23:36,381 --> 00:23:40,152
You used to trust him so much,
just because he proposed to you.

398
00:23:40,719 --> 00:23:42,988
Without rhyme or reason,

399
00:23:43,054 --> 00:23:45,190
you ingenuously placed your faith in him.

400
00:23:50,595 --> 00:23:51,430
I'm sorry.

401
00:23:54,332 --> 00:23:57,202
I know I'm free to believe what I want.

402
00:23:58,970 --> 00:24:01,006
But the way you see it,

403
00:24:01,973 --> 00:24:04,376
I suppose it's all wishful thinking.

404
00:24:06,344 --> 00:24:08,713
I don't know all the details yet,

405
00:24:08,780 --> 00:24:11,817
but there's no denying
that Mitsuru was with your wife.

406
00:24:13,985 --> 00:24:15,754
And yet, only he survived.

407
00:24:21,293 --> 00:24:22,260
I'm so sorry.

408
00:24:24,596 --> 00:24:27,132
There's no use apologizing for that.

409
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:30,101
I'm so sorry.

410
00:24:35,740 --> 00:24:38,810
Let's find him
and make him apologize in person.

411
00:24:39,711 --> 00:24:41,480
So, what should we do next?

412
00:24:42,113 --> 00:24:45,417
Maybe put up a flyer?
You see those for lost pets all the time--

413
00:24:45,484 --> 00:24:46,785
I'm so sorry.

414
00:24:52,023 --> 00:24:53,592
I do feel like

415
00:24:54,893 --> 00:24:57,696
giving Mitsuru a big "Screw you!"

416
00:24:57,762 --> 00:25:02,200
but you didn't do anything wrong,
so no need to apologize.

417
00:25:02,267 --> 00:25:03,435
He's my family.

418
00:25:06,137 --> 00:25:07,205
I'm his wife.

419
00:25:08,640 --> 00:25:10,308
So, I'm responsible, too.

420
00:25:13,879 --> 00:25:15,413
I knew someone from college,

421
00:25:16,615 --> 00:25:17,816
the same year as mine,

422
00:25:19,317 --> 00:25:22,621
who struggled with job hunting
because his brother had a criminal record.

423
00:25:24,556 --> 00:25:27,759
I always thought it was weird,
because he was a fine guy.

424
00:25:27,826 --> 00:25:29,861
He had cut ties with his junkie brother,

425
00:25:29,928 --> 00:25:31,963
and of course, never did drugs himself.

426
00:25:33,632 --> 00:25:36,601
Maybe that wasn't the best example.

427
00:25:37,402 --> 00:25:41,439
Anyway, I won't take
my anger toward Mitsuru out on you.

428
00:25:45,677 --> 00:25:47,012
If you find him,

429
00:25:47,913 --> 00:25:49,047
you go first.

430
00:25:50,749 --> 00:25:53,251
You mean, punch him? Me first?

431
00:25:53,318 --> 00:25:54,319
Yes.

432
00:25:54,386 --> 00:25:56,755
Suddenly, I'm feeling more motivated.

433
00:25:57,455 --> 00:25:59,457
I'll do whatever it takes to find him.

434
00:26:00,592 --> 00:26:01,426
Yes.

435
00:26:02,794 --> 00:26:03,728
What about you?

436
00:26:05,030 --> 00:26:06,565
What's your passcode?

437
00:26:07,332 --> 00:26:10,335
I already gave mine. Just spill it.

438
00:26:11,770 --> 00:26:13,038
It's 0317.

439
00:26:14,673 --> 00:26:16,541
Mitsuru's birthday.

440
00:26:19,711 --> 00:26:20,979
You really love him.

441
00:26:21,947 --> 00:26:24,849
"How to find a missing person."

442
00:26:26,051 --> 00:26:27,586
The police…

443
00:26:27,652 --> 00:26:29,588
A private investigator…

444
00:26:31,590 --> 00:26:32,824
Or through social media--

445
00:26:33,625 --> 00:26:35,060
Oh, no!

446
00:26:37,696 --> 00:26:38,630
Geez.

447
00:26:50,308 --> 00:26:52,010
Oh, dear.

448
00:26:52,077 --> 00:26:54,279
What will you do, now that I'm gone?

449
00:26:54,346 --> 00:26:55,547
Can you get that stain out?

450
00:26:56,147 --> 00:26:57,482
No, I can't.

451
00:26:58,383 --> 00:27:00,852
Why don't you just

452
00:27:00,919 --> 00:27:02,921
cut out the stained part?

453
00:27:02,988 --> 00:27:03,822
Really?

454
00:27:03,888 --> 00:27:06,324
So it'll show a little skin
and make it sexy?

455
00:27:06,391 --> 00:27:08,493
I don't know what you're talking about.

456
00:27:09,527 --> 00:27:10,962
You know,

457
00:27:11,663 --> 00:27:13,999
the T-shirt you gave me?

458
00:27:14,065 --> 00:27:14,899
Yes.

459
00:27:14,966 --> 00:27:16,635
It's fantastic.

460
00:27:16,701 --> 00:27:19,604
You're the pickiest man in Japan
when it comes to T-shirts.

461
00:27:19,671 --> 00:27:21,339
And this one is perfect.

462
00:27:22,207 --> 00:27:23,174
The size…

463
00:27:25,110 --> 00:27:28,279
the thickness of the fabric,
how snug the neckline is…

464
00:27:28,346 --> 00:27:29,180
Isn't it?

465
00:27:29,247 --> 00:27:30,348
Where did you buy it?

466
00:27:30,415 --> 00:27:31,483
Online.

467
00:27:31,549 --> 00:27:34,486
You can only get it
from the brand's official online store.

468
00:27:34,552 --> 00:27:36,821
It takes some time to arrive
after ordering.

469
00:27:36,888 --> 00:27:38,189
That type of shop.

470
00:27:38,256 --> 00:27:40,125
Maybe I should stock up on some of this.

471
00:27:45,764 --> 00:27:46,765
Coming.

472
00:27:54,839 --> 00:27:56,941
-Delivery.
-Sure.

473
00:27:57,008 --> 00:27:58,276
Thank you, sir.

474
00:28:10,255 --> 00:28:11,756
It's for you, Azusa.

475
00:28:15,860 --> 00:28:17,295
What did you buy?

476
00:28:31,142 --> 00:28:32,577
I'll just open this, okay?

477
00:29:06,878 --> 00:29:08,980
Sorry, that's your favorite…

478
00:29:23,428 --> 00:29:26,197
48TH MUSASHINO
SUMMER FIREWORKS FESTIVAL

479
00:29:26,831 --> 00:29:28,333
A fireworks festival?

480
00:29:34,439 --> 00:29:37,375
COUPLE'S SEAT

481
00:29:43,782 --> 00:29:45,283
-Good morning.
-Morning.

482
00:29:49,220 --> 00:29:50,421
Chizuru.

483
00:29:50,488 --> 00:29:51,456
Yes?

484
00:29:51,990 --> 00:29:53,224
Perhaps it's time

485
00:29:53,291 --> 00:29:56,294
you entrusted me with a major project.

486
00:29:57,095 --> 00:29:58,196
I'm kind of idle.

487
00:29:58,263 --> 00:30:01,065
Everyone else seems so busy,
and I feel kind of guilty.

488
00:30:01,132 --> 00:30:02,133
Well…

489
00:30:05,503 --> 00:30:07,505
If that's what you want, Sakiko.

490
00:30:08,339 --> 00:30:09,707
Thank you.

491
00:30:09,774 --> 00:30:11,843
It's okay. I know he will come back.

492
00:30:13,344 --> 00:30:14,445
Okay.

493
00:30:14,512 --> 00:30:15,747
Hey, Rino.

494
00:30:15,814 --> 00:30:16,648
Yes?

495
00:30:16,714 --> 00:30:19,717
I have tickets for the fireworks festival.
You can go with your partner.

496
00:30:19,784 --> 00:30:21,653
They're for so-called "couple's seats."

497
00:30:21,719 --> 00:30:22,921
Really?

498
00:30:22,987 --> 00:30:24,489
I would love to go.

499
00:30:24,556 --> 00:30:27,192
Great. Here you go.

500
00:30:27,258 --> 00:30:28,259
SUMMER FIREWORKS FESTIVAL

501
00:30:29,761 --> 00:30:32,163
Oh, dear. I'm so sorry.

502
00:30:32,230 --> 00:30:35,333
I already have plans for that day,
so I can't go.

503
00:30:35,400 --> 00:30:36,968
I see. Got it.

504
00:30:37,869 --> 00:30:40,605
Why would you have tickets
to a fireworks festival?

505
00:30:42,140 --> 00:30:44,442
I found them inside Mitsuru's jacket.

506
00:30:45,009 --> 00:30:45,910
What?

507
00:30:45,977 --> 00:30:48,112
Tickets for next month's festival.

508
00:30:48,179 --> 00:30:50,648
Two of them. Couple's seats.

509
00:30:51,616 --> 00:30:53,151
Wow.

510
00:30:55,954 --> 00:30:57,822
-Hey.
-What?

511
00:30:57,889 --> 00:31:00,525
If there's something
you don't want your wife to see,

512
00:31:00,592 --> 00:31:01,993
where would you hide it?

513
00:31:02,060 --> 00:31:03,728
I don't have anything like that.

514
00:31:04,329 --> 00:31:06,331
I knew it.

515
00:31:06,397 --> 00:31:07,332
Why did you ask?

516
00:31:08,433 --> 00:31:09,601
Well…

517
00:31:10,468 --> 00:31:12,237
-In that case…
-Yes?

518
00:31:12,871 --> 00:31:15,607
What if you want to go
somewhere your wife doesn't like,

519
00:31:15,673 --> 00:31:17,141
but you would feel awkward going alone?

520
00:31:17,208 --> 00:31:18,376
Well…

521
00:31:19,477 --> 00:31:20,511
I would go with you.

522
00:31:20,578 --> 00:31:22,347
I thought so too!

523
00:31:22,947 --> 00:31:24,782
That is so not helpful.

524
00:31:24,849 --> 00:31:29,687
Don't you think so-called normal couples
end up on bad terms with each other

525
00:31:29,754 --> 00:31:33,024
because they make each other
hide things from one another?

526
00:31:34,425 --> 00:31:36,728
Oh. Good point.

527
00:31:45,403 --> 00:31:46,304
Sakiko.

528
00:31:46,371 --> 00:31:49,574
That's all I need.
Just find a good spot to leave off.

529
00:31:49,641 --> 00:31:51,342
So, I'll do it until there.

530
00:31:51,409 --> 00:31:53,144
Thank you.

531
00:31:54,879 --> 00:31:57,515
Since you can't go and hire a part-timer
all by yourself.

532
00:31:57,582 --> 00:31:59,217
Yeah, that's true.

533
00:32:00,785 --> 00:32:02,353
Maybe I should just hire you.

534
00:32:02,420 --> 00:32:03,922
Go ahead and hire me.

535
00:32:03,988 --> 00:32:06,057
Go ahead, just take over the shop.

536
00:32:11,930 --> 00:32:13,765
Sorry about my husband.

537
00:32:16,467 --> 00:32:18,836
It's tough being married, isn't it?

538
00:32:19,704 --> 00:32:21,572
You didn't do anything wrong, Sakiko.

539
00:32:22,373 --> 00:32:24,876
It can't be helped.
We're family, after all.

540
00:32:27,512 --> 00:32:30,148
Family, or rather, a married couple?

541
00:32:31,182 --> 00:32:32,016
Yes.

542
00:32:33,117 --> 00:32:33,952
Wait.

543
00:32:34,485 --> 00:32:36,554
A married couple is a family, right?

544
00:32:36,621 --> 00:32:39,324
But you were strangers to begin with.

545
00:32:39,390 --> 00:32:42,093
And now, we're a married couple.

546
00:32:42,961 --> 00:32:44,329
Not strangers anymore.

547
00:32:51,569 --> 00:32:53,404
I left the sign out front.

548
00:32:57,308 --> 00:32:58,242
Hold on.

549
00:32:58,309 --> 00:32:59,177
Got it.

550
00:33:01,646 --> 00:33:03,147
Hello, this is Café Hikoki.

551
00:33:05,149 --> 00:33:06,117
Hello?

552
00:33:14,359 --> 00:33:15,326
What?

553
00:33:16,761 --> 00:33:17,829
They hung up

554
00:33:18,396 --> 00:33:19,397
I see.

555
00:33:20,198 --> 00:33:22,867
That happens sometimes. Prank calls.

556
00:33:22,934 --> 00:33:25,169
My, that's awful.

557
00:33:35,913 --> 00:33:36,914
Naoto.

558
00:33:37,582 --> 00:33:38,583
Yes?

559
00:33:38,649 --> 00:33:40,685
Can I take Mitsuru's apron home?

560
00:33:41,352 --> 00:33:42,687
I want to wash it.

561
00:34:04,375 --> 00:34:05,543
Where's the kid?

562
00:34:05,610 --> 00:34:08,179
My mother is caring for him at her place.

563
00:34:08,780 --> 00:34:10,248
I see.

564
00:34:11,582 --> 00:34:13,184
She's supportive.

565
00:34:13,251 --> 00:34:14,852
What a wonderful family.

566
00:34:23,895 --> 00:34:26,063
This one, isn't it cute?

567
00:34:27,565 --> 00:34:28,800
It was Azusa

568
00:34:29,767 --> 00:34:31,436
who gave it to me.

569
00:34:32,870 --> 00:34:36,140
She said that old people
could actually die from the heat.

570
00:34:37,975 --> 00:34:39,410
Azusa once said,

571
00:34:40,144 --> 00:34:42,547
it was so weird
that she thought it was broken,

572
00:34:43,081 --> 00:34:44,515
so she gave it away.

573
00:34:46,150 --> 00:34:47,251
Guess who got it.

574
00:34:47,318 --> 00:34:48,519
Really…

575
00:34:51,155 --> 00:34:51,989
Right.

576
00:34:53,691 --> 00:34:54,625
These

577
00:34:55,159 --> 00:34:58,663
are your wife's belongings
that she left at the store.

578
00:34:58,729 --> 00:34:59,597
Here you go.

579
00:34:59,664 --> 00:35:01,566
Right. Sorry you had to do this.

580
00:35:01,632 --> 00:35:02,900
No, it's no big deal.

581
00:35:03,701 --> 00:35:05,136
I could've taken them that day.

582
00:35:05,203 --> 00:35:07,672
No, I hadn't sorted through them yet.

583
00:35:08,573 --> 00:35:10,608
Besides that,

584
00:35:11,409 --> 00:35:14,712
I still needed some time
to sort out my own feelings.

585
00:35:18,416 --> 00:35:21,652
ENTRY PASS

586
00:35:26,290 --> 00:35:27,358
The aquarium.

587
00:35:28,893 --> 00:35:29,894
Yes.

588
00:35:30,561 --> 00:35:34,432
Azusa was a fan of aquariums, wasn't she?

589
00:35:34,499 --> 00:35:36,400
Even during her break,

590
00:35:36,467 --> 00:35:39,437
she would constantly
look up all sorts of places.

591
00:35:45,610 --> 00:35:48,045
-What, is something off?
-What?

592
00:35:48,112 --> 00:35:49,147
No.

593
00:35:49,847 --> 00:35:52,483
Is it weird for a woman
to want to go to the aquarium?

594
00:35:52,550 --> 00:35:53,384
No…

595
00:35:54,051 --> 00:35:55,887
Right, there were two tickets.

596
00:35:56,821 --> 00:36:00,625
Perhaps she was planning to go with you.

597
00:36:01,692 --> 00:36:03,327
There aren't any kids' tickets.

598
00:36:03,394 --> 00:36:06,230
Maybe you just wanted
to spend time alone together.

599
00:36:06,297 --> 00:36:07,165
Oh, tea.

600
00:36:07,231 --> 00:36:08,599
Or do you prefer coffee?

601
00:36:08,666 --> 00:36:10,067
-Yes.
-Got it.

602
00:36:16,073 --> 00:36:18,309
About this mini fan.

603
00:36:19,677 --> 00:36:20,578
Yes?

604
00:36:21,479 --> 00:36:23,080
Why didn't you ask her,

605
00:36:23,147 --> 00:36:26,918
"Who did you give it to?" or something?
Weren't you curious?

606
00:36:26,984 --> 00:36:30,054
No, my wife would bring up things
she wanted to talk about on her own.

607
00:36:32,423 --> 00:36:35,092
You have no idea
what she was like at home.

608
00:36:35,726 --> 00:36:38,329
There was a certain distance
between us as a couple.

609
00:36:38,396 --> 00:36:40,198
Yeah, I can see that.

610
00:36:40,264 --> 00:36:43,968
My apologies, it looks like
this old man's getting a little too nosy.

611
00:36:44,035 --> 00:36:47,138
Her catchphrase at home was,
"Hey, Itsuki, listen."

612
00:36:47,205 --> 00:36:49,941
When I got home first, for instance,

613
00:36:50,007 --> 00:36:53,945
she'd start talking about almost anything
before I even closed the front door.

614
00:36:54,011 --> 00:36:55,046
I see.

615
00:36:55,112 --> 00:36:56,781
She would often tell me after the fact.

616
00:36:56,847 --> 00:36:59,383
Sometimes I would ask,
"You went there?" or "You bought that?"

617
00:37:00,284 --> 00:37:03,254
That kind of spontaneous,
free-spirited vibe.

618
00:37:04,255 --> 00:37:05,890
That was Azusa at home.

619
00:37:06,824 --> 00:37:07,959
I see.

620
00:37:08,025 --> 00:37:09,327
I'm pretty sure

621
00:37:10,261 --> 00:37:12,563
she would have told me about it that day.

622
00:37:12,630 --> 00:37:14,065
"Hey, Itsuki, listen."

623
00:37:14,899 --> 00:37:17,468
"Today, I joined one of our neighbors,

624
00:37:18,569 --> 00:37:22,173
that is Mr. Seo, on a bus trip to Nagano."

625
00:37:22,240 --> 00:37:23,674
And?

626
00:37:23,741 --> 00:37:25,176
"You see,

627
00:37:25,243 --> 00:37:28,279
I met Mr. Seo about half a year ago
at the coin laundry, and…"

628
00:37:28,846 --> 00:37:31,816
We often chat
while waiting for the laundry to finish.

629
00:37:32,583 --> 00:37:34,785
I told you about it, right, Itsuki?

630
00:37:34,852 --> 00:37:37,822
That delicious soba restaurant
in Nagano I saw on <i>Brunch.</i>

631
00:37:37,888 --> 00:37:40,191
When I mentioned it to Mr. Seo,

632
00:37:40,258 --> 00:37:43,594
he was like,
"I've been wanting to go there too."

633
00:37:43,661 --> 00:37:46,297
and then I said, "Oh, let's go together."

634
00:37:49,800 --> 00:37:50,735
Sorry.

635
00:37:51,435 --> 00:37:53,771
Sometimes I just want to
get away from Tokyo,

636
00:37:53,838 --> 00:37:56,841
get out in nature and relax, for a change.

637
00:37:57,742 --> 00:38:00,978
I meant to inform you before I left,

638
00:38:01,045 --> 00:38:04,749
but Miki had just offered
to hang out with Sho.

639
00:38:04,815 --> 00:38:08,152
And for me to go out with another man
at such a time…

640
00:38:09,353 --> 00:38:12,923
I feared if I told your mom about that,
she might get the wrong idea.

641
00:38:13,557 --> 00:38:14,759
Or so I overthought.

642
00:38:15,293 --> 00:38:17,328
So I had to tell you after the fact.

643
00:38:18,929 --> 00:38:20,364
But…

644
00:38:20,431 --> 00:38:23,768
Yes, he's a man, but there's really
nothing going on between us.

645
00:38:23,834 --> 00:38:26,170
We just chat once a month
at the coin laundry.

646
00:38:26,237 --> 00:38:28,572
Today was the first time we met
somewhere else.

647
00:38:28,639 --> 00:38:29,507
Oh…

648
00:38:29,573 --> 00:38:31,776
We did sit next to each other on the bus.

649
00:38:32,810 --> 00:38:34,578
I'm really sorry about that.

650
00:38:35,246 --> 00:38:37,548
"I thought you would believe me, Itsuki,

651
00:38:37,615 --> 00:38:39,350
so I'm going to tell you the truth.

652
00:38:39,417 --> 00:38:40,951
You'll believe me, right?"

653
00:38:43,587 --> 00:38:45,256
That's what she would say.

654
00:38:46,457 --> 00:38:47,291
Wow.

655
00:38:47,925 --> 00:38:50,161
-That was wonderful. Thank you.
-Yes.

656
00:38:50,227 --> 00:38:51,629
It was entertaining.

657
00:38:51,696 --> 00:38:52,930
Well…

658
00:38:52,997 --> 00:38:54,498
You don't mind barley tea?

659
00:38:56,033 --> 00:38:58,869
Well then, in that case,

660
00:38:58,936 --> 00:39:01,806
I'm taking back what I said earlier.

661
00:39:01,872 --> 00:39:06,844
I just thought it wasn't right
to twist the deceased's past

662
00:39:06,911 --> 00:39:11,048
for the sake of those left behind.

663
00:39:11,115 --> 00:39:13,384
Let me just say this.

664
00:39:14,752 --> 00:39:15,586
Yes?

665
00:39:15,653 --> 00:39:20,291
We used to chat a lot
while we were working.

666
00:39:21,292 --> 00:39:22,393
Well…

667
00:39:22,460 --> 00:39:23,828
Back then,

668
00:39:25,262 --> 00:39:27,064
your wife would love to say…

669
00:39:29,100 --> 00:39:30,534
"Hey, boss."

670
00:39:31,802 --> 00:39:36,073
"Listen, I can't tell my husband this."

671
00:39:37,942 --> 00:39:39,009
That's all.

672
00:39:49,286 --> 00:39:50,955
I have to go now. Bye.

673
00:40:01,198 --> 00:40:02,933
<i>Maybe I'm using Azusa's death</i>

674
00:40:03,534 --> 00:40:05,703
<i>as an excuse to romanticize</i>

675
00:40:05,770 --> 00:40:07,772
<i>both myself and her too much.</i>

676
00:40:09,507 --> 00:40:11,642
SHINAGAWA AQUARIUM
ENTRY PASS

677
00:40:13,577 --> 00:40:16,147
<i>I just simply hate aquariums.</i>

678
00:40:24,855 --> 00:40:26,457
<i>Azusa knew that</i>

679
00:40:26,524 --> 00:40:27,958
<i>really well.</i>

680
00:40:36,634 --> 00:40:39,270
<i>I just simply hate fireworks festivals.</i>

681
00:40:50,648 --> 00:40:52,082
<i>Mitsuru knows that</i>

682
00:40:53,117 --> 00:40:54,318
<i>really well.</i>

683
00:41:03,194 --> 00:41:04,028
Wow.

684
00:41:04,595 --> 00:41:06,430
Maki is getting married.

685
00:41:15,005 --> 00:41:16,674
Our wedding date…

686
00:41:18,542 --> 00:41:20,845
June 28th…

687
00:41:48,072 --> 00:41:50,875
<i>I felt bad about</i>
<i>breaking into Mitsuru's phone,</i>

688
00:41:50,941 --> 00:41:53,978
<i>worried that he might get mad</i>
<i>when he comes home.</i>

689
00:42:14,865 --> 00:42:16,433
<i>But it's okay.</i>

690
00:42:18,135 --> 00:42:19,370
<i>He probably…</i>

691
00:42:21,171 --> 00:42:22,840
This is Café Hikoki.

692
00:42:23,641 --> 00:42:24,808
<i>…won't come home.</i>

693
00:42:25,543 --> 00:42:26,377
Hello?

694
00:42:27,745 --> 00:42:28,746
<i>I'm sorry.</i>

695
00:42:33,918 --> 00:42:36,520
That happens sometimes. Prank calls.

696
00:42:36,587 --> 00:42:38,656
My, that's awful.

697
00:42:44,762 --> 00:42:45,796
<i>Mitsuru…</i>

698
00:42:48,065 --> 00:42:49,366
<i>isn't coming back.</i>

699
00:42:55,372 --> 00:42:57,741
Apparently, she went to the service area.

700
00:42:58,475 --> 00:43:00,010
With that other guy.

701
00:43:01,645 --> 00:43:04,014
It's going to be all right.

702
00:43:05,115 --> 00:43:07,351
It seems she's even able to go to work.

703
00:43:08,452 --> 00:43:10,588
It may have been a coincidence,

704
00:43:10,654 --> 00:43:13,257
but aren't you glad you got to
hear her voice a little?

705
00:43:14,191 --> 00:43:16,193
It made me really nervous, though.

706
00:43:19,430 --> 00:43:22,766
If you're that worried,
why don't you just come home,

707
00:43:23,901 --> 00:43:24,935
Mr. Seo?

708
00:43:55,633 --> 00:43:58,936
Subtitle translation by: Frederick
